The attached image, Figure 11 from the Journal of Controlled Release (2015) paper, illustrates the difference in residual inflammation at injection sites two weeks after intramuscular injection of adjuvants. The left panel shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (indicated by asterisks) in skeletal muscle (M) induced by aluminum hydroxide adjuvant. In contrast, the right panel shows much less inflammation at the Nano-11 injection site, with relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords within the connective tissue between muscle fibers. This demonstrates that Nano-11 induces a more transient and quicker-rescuing inflammatory response compared to aluminum hydroxide, supporting its excellent safety profile as a vaccine adjuvant.